delete the level files. Recover is not possible in this case.

> IMO this is not save scumming because I was using ONE hand to type, and did 
> not hit the "Y" key at all.  Does that question to confirm a Quit respond 
> to any keys OTHER than Y or N??

Accidentally #quit'ing and restoring the fieles is something I'd not
count as cheating.

My version (Unix/tty) does not seem to be sensible to ^Q but to ^C
which has to be confirmed by either y or n. The behaviour could be
different on your platform (I did not check the other platforms I/O).

In the first instance the code checks only for "no" as response

   if(yn("Really quit?") == 'n')

thus seems to allow every other response to confirm the #quit. The
function yn() is therefore responsible to accept just y and n, and
if it misbehaves accepting more than these two choices, it could
result in the observed behaviour.

Being paranoid and not wanting to rely on the correctness of the
underlying function it would have been safer if the code would in
this case compare  yn() != 'y'  instead.

